```json
{
  "root_causes": [
    {
      "service": "profile",
      "fault_kind": "pod_unavailable",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "kind": "metric",
          "sql": "SELECT metric, attr_k8s_pod_name AS pod, count(*) AS n, round(min(value),2) AS min_v, round(max(value),2) AS max_v FROM abnormal_metrics WHERE metric='k8s.container.restarts' AND attr_k8s_pod_name LIKE 'profile%' GROUP BY 1,2",
          "claim": "Profile pod restart counter rises to 6 in the incident window; it was 0 throughout the normal period."
        },
        {
          "kind": "metric",
          "sql": "SELECT metric, attr_k8s_deployment_name AS dep, count(*) AS n, round(avg(value),2) AS avg_v, round(max(value),2) AS max_v FROM abnormal_metrics WHERE attr_k8s_deployment_name='profile' AND metric='k8s.deployment.available' GROUP BY 1,2",
          "claim": "profile deployment reports zero available replicas across the incident window, while normal period shows 1.0."
        },
        {
          "kind": "trace",
          "sql": "SELECT service_name, attr_span_kind, span_name, count(*) AS n FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='profile' GROUP BY 1,2,3 UNION ALL SELECT service_name, attr_span_kind, span_name, count(*) FROM normal_traces WHERE service_name='profile' GROUP BY 1,2,3",
          "claim": "Profile emits no spans during the incident, while the normal period has about 1,184 GetProfiles server spans."
        },
        {
          "kind": "log",
          "sql": "SELECT service_name, level, message, count(*) AS n FROM abnormal_logs WHERE service_name='frontend' AND level='ERROR' GROUP BY 1,2,3",
          "claim": "Frontend logs 607 'Get profiles failed' ERRORs during the incident; none appear in the normal period."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "propagation": [
    {
      "from": "profile",
      "to": "frontend",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "kind": "trace",
          "sql": "WITH f AS (SELECT trace_id, span_name, attr_status_code st FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='frontend' AND attr_span_kind='Server') SELECT f.span_name AS endpoint, f.st, c.span_name AS child, c.attr_status_code AS child_status, count(*) AS n FROM f LEFT JOIN abnormal_traces c ON c.trace_id=f.trace_id AND c.attr_span_kind='Client' AND c.service_name='frontend' GROUP BY 1,2,3,4 ORDER BY 1,3",
          "claim": "Frontend /hotels (518) and /recommendations (338) return Error exactly when their profile.GetProfiles call errors; other endpoints stay clean."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

**Summary:** The user-visible degradation is confined to the `/hotels` and `/recommendations` endpoints on `frontend`. Both depend on `profile.GetProfiles`, which errors 856 times. The profile pod restarted repeatedly and had no available replicas, and it emitted no spans or logs during the incident. The fault is therefore the profile service going down, which I labelled `pod_unavailable`; it is in the same equivalence class as `pod_failure`. I found no network (`hubble_*`) metrics in this case and no other service with error-status spans, so no other root cause is named. Other services' span counts shifted with traffic, but their latencies were flat.
